---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro'
import Command from '../../components/Command.astro'
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro'
import { blocks, components } from '../../data/catalog'

const vueMain = `// src/main.ts
import './styles/ermine.css'`
const vueUse = `<script setup lang="ts">
import { Button } from '@/components/ui/button'
</script>

<template>
  <Button variant="outline">Save</Button>
</template>`
const nuxtConfig = `// nuxt.config.ts
export default defineNuxtConfig({
  css: ['~/assets/css/ermine.css'],
  components: [
    // Auto-import the components by their own names: <Button>, <DialogContent>, ...
    { path: '~/components/ui', pathPrefix: false, extensions: ['.vue'] },
    // The same for blocks, if you add any: <Dashboard>, <SignIn>, ...
    { path: '~/components/blocks', pathPrefix: false, extensions: ['.vue'] },
    '~/components',
  ],
})`
const astroPage = `---
import '../styles/ermine.css'
import SettingsForm from '../components/SettingsForm.vue'
---
<SettingsForm client:load />`
const theme = `:root {
  --er-primary: light-dark(oklch(0.5 0.2 265), oklch(0.75 0.15 265));
  --er-radius: 0.375rem;
}`
const override = `.er-button[data-variant='outline'] { border-style: dashed; }`
const config = `{
  "components": "src/components/ui",
  "lib": "src/lib/ermine",
  "styles": "src/styles/ermine.css"
}`
---

<Layout title="Installation. Ermine UI" description="Add Ermine UI to a Vue, Nuxt or Astro project: set up once, then copy in the components you want.">
  <div class="wrap doc">
    <header class="doc-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Installation</p>
      <h1 class="headline">Two commands.</h1>
      <p class="lede">Set up once, then add the components you want. Their source is copied into your project; nothing is imported from <code>node_modules</code>.</p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>You need a Vue 3.5, Nuxt 4 or Astro project and Node 18 or later. The browsers need to be from 2024 onward, because the CSS uses <code>light-dark()</code>, <code>@layer</code>, <code>color-mix()</code> and <code>oklch()</code>.</p>

      <h2 id="set-up">1. Set up</h2>
      <p>Run this in your project's folder. It detects the framework, suggests folders, writes <code>ermine.json</code> and copies the theme stylesheet. Add <code>-y</code> to accept the suggested folders without being asked.</p>
      <Command args="init" />
      <CodeBlock code={config} lang="json" label="ermine.json" />

      <h2 id="add">2. Add components</h2>
      <p>Name as many as you like. Each brings the helpers and other components it depends on. Files that already exist are kept unless you pass <code>--overwrite</code>.</p>
      <Command args={['add button dialog select', 'add dashboard']} />
      <p>
        The second line adds a block: a section of a page, already put together, along with every component it uses. Blocks go in a <code>blocks</code> folder beside your components. Browse{' '}
        <a href="/components/">all {components.length} components and {blocks.length} blocks</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="frameworks">3. Load the stylesheet and use them</h2>

      <h3 id="vue">Vue with Vite</h3>
      <CodeBlock code={vueMain} lang="ts" />
      <CodeBlock code={vueUse} lang="vue" />

      <h3 id="nuxt">Nuxt</h3>
      <CodeBlock code={nuxtConfig} lang="ts" />
      <p>Components are then available in templates without imports. <code>toast</code> is a function, so import it: <code>import {'{ toast }'} from '~/components/ui/toast'</code>.</p>

      <h3 id="astro">Astro</h3>
      <p>Add the Vue integration with <code>npx astro add vue</code>, import the stylesheet in a layout, and use the components inside a Vue island.</p>
      <CodeBlock code={astroPage} lang="astro" />
      <p>
        Compound components such as dialog, select and tabs share state between their parts, so compose them inside one <code>.vue</code> file and not across separate islands. Static ones such as <code>Button</code>, <code>Badge</code> and <code>Card</code> can be used directly in{' '}
        <code>.astro</code> files with no client directive.
      </p>

      <h2 id="commands">Commands</h2>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>ermine init</code></td><td>Create <code>ermine.json</code> and the theme stylesheet. <code>-y</code> accepts the suggested folders.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>ermine add &lt;name...&gt;</code></td><td>Copy components or blocks and what they depend on. <code>--all</code> copies every component.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>ermine list</code></td><td>Show what is available and which you already have.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>--overwrite</code></td><td>Replace files that already exist.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>--cwd &lt;dir&gt;</code></td><td>Run as if started in another folder.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>--registry &lt;url or path&gt;</code></td><td>Read components from a different <code>registry.json</code>, such as your own fork.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2 id="theming">Theming</h2>
      <p><code>ermine.css</code> defines every colour, radius, shadow and duration as a CSS variable. Edit the values and every component follows. The file is yours and is never overwritten, even with <code>--overwrite</code>.</p>
      <CodeBlock code={theme} lang="css" />
      <p>Or start from a ready-made <a href="/themes/">colour theme</a>: a base for the greys and an accent for the primary colour, each checked for contrast in light and dark.</p>
      <Command args="theme slate blue" />
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Dark mode</strong> follows the operating system. To force a theme, put <code>class="dark"</code> or <code>class="light"</code> on <code>&lt;html&gt;</code>, or on any element to theme only what is inside it.</li>
        <li><strong>Overriding a component.</strong> Component styles sit in <code>@layer components</code>, so any rule you write outside a layer wins without <code>!important</code>. Or edit the component's own <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> block; it is your code.</li>
        <li><strong>With Tailwind.</strong> The layer has the same name Tailwind uses, so utility classes on an Ermine component override its defaults.</li>
      </ul>
      <CodeBlock code={override} lang="css" />

      <h2 id="by-hand">Installing by hand</h2>
      <p>The command line is a convenience. Every <a href="/components/">component page</a> shows the source of each file and where it goes, so you can create them yourself.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
